ity like the regulation  is intense it drags on forever after you  get done with it the Sierra Club sues  you and then they sue again it's it's  not conducive to investment at all on  the other hand though like when when  it's there they all every group went  through this and they ended up being far  more LNG processing plants approved in  the United States than were ever  economically feasible and the other part  of this of course is that it's not  simply the government's fault a lot of  these regulations as you mentioned the  the big cheerleaders of them are the  private sector are the established  players in the private sector and the  only way to roll them back would be  through the state  so again I think it comes down to more  intelligent use of the state rather than  sort of getting rid of it we have time  for one final question  do you see a correlation or just a  coincidence between the decline in  American society and the decline  economically you're describing here in  other words the American families are  falling apart the decline in educational  attainment the decline in the value of  the perceived value of work and working  hard and being responsible and all those  sort of family values that are falling  apart  mass shootings by psychopaths every  other week and so on is that correlated  or is that just coincident with what  you're describing  I think it's correlated and I don't you  know people can get into a big debate  and endless debate about you know our  family's falling apart because people  can't find jobs or our people not  finding jobs because there are no  families to instill work ethic and basic  mores and things like that I don't  really know what the answer to that  question is but I think they are related  the other thing you know we are living  in a society that has basically the  bureaucracy of Brezhnev and the  oligarchs of Yeltsin and a big problem  is that no one actually wants to think  in terms of in any sort of political  community any kind of national interests  you know any discussion of national  interest national goals this this is you  know alarming not only to the left but  to significant parts of the right now  and and that I think is a is a major  cultural change because it until you can  begin to think about sort of you know  larger national interests it's very hard  to address these problems and and like I  said the left sort of the left gets a  lot of the economics of it but they  refuse to actually think about you know  anything that might benefit the United  States which they're frightened of the  right  wants to help the United States but many  of them are well actually shouldn't say  that half the right is fine with the  United States half of it is more  concerned about the market but even  among the half that is fine with the  United States there's an increasing  reluctance to sort of think through the  economics so so that's another I think  you know a deep cultural problem the  Chinese don't have that problem  incidentally but that's a deep cultural  issue that makes it very difficult to  really even talk about this stuff in an  intelligent way and like I said it makes  the allure of free trade everyone's  always winning all the time very  attractive  thanks a lot  [Applause]
 

